##
    What the compiler knows about an optional, and where it stops knowing it.

    An optional cannot be read as a plain value until something proves it holds one. Once
    something has, it reads as the value for as long as the proof lasts — that is narrowing,
    and it is why a guarded block does not have to unwrap the same name twice.

    A proof lasts until control reaches a point that could have been reached another way. This
    file is mostly about where those points are, because that is the part worth knowing: the
    proof itself is one line, and where it stops is the rest.

    The refusals are in samples/negatives/compile/narrowing.pc, which is the same list written
    the other way up.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.CheckingIt();
        Program.LeavingEarly();
        Program.EveryWayThrough();
        Program.WhereItStops();
        Program.WhatAClosureTakesAway();
    end function

    # ---- Checking it -------------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: A check proves it for the block it guards.

        @remarks: Inside the block the name reads as an integer, so it adds and prints
        without unwrapping. Outside it, it is an optional again — the check said nothing
        about out there.

        @value: one that may hold nothing.
    ##
    function Describe(integer? value)
        if value.HasValue()
            Console.WriteLine("  holding " + (value + 1) + " once one is added");
        else
            Console.WriteLine("  holding nothing");
        end if
    end function

    # @summary: Shows a check proving a value for the block it guards.
    function CheckingIt()
        Console.WriteLine("== checking it ==");

        Program.Describe(41);
        Program.Describe(Program.Nothing());
    end function

    # ---- Leaving early -----------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: The first even number in a set, doubled, or zero where there is none.

        @remarks: The guard leaves rather than wrapping the rest of the function in a block.
        An arm that always leaves never arrives at the line below it, so the only way to be
        standing there is the other one — and the compiler reads it that way. This is the
        shape most guards want, and it is why the body is not indented one level per check.

        @values: the numbers to look through.
        @yields: twice the first even one, or zero.
    ##
    integer function TwiceTheFirstEven(integer[] values)
        integer? found;

        loop each value in values
            if value % 2 == 0
                found = value;
                break;
            end if
        end loop

        # Nothing a loop stored is narrowed after it, so this is where the proof is made.
        if not found.HasValue()
            yield 0;
        end if

        yield found * 2;
    end function

    # @summary: Shows a guard that leaves, rather than one that wraps.
    function LeavingEarly()
        Console.WriteLine("== leaving early ==");

        Console.WriteLine("  {7, 3, 12, 5} -> " + Program.TwiceTheFirstEven({7, 3, 12, 5}));
        Console.WriteLine("  {7, 3, 5}     -> " + Program.TwiceTheFirstEven({7, 3, 5}));
    end function

    # ---- Every way through -------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: A label for a score, where every arm settles on one.

        @remarks: Storing a value proves presence exactly as a check does. What makes it hold
        after the branch is that every way through stores one — including the way where no arm
        runs at all, which is why a chain with no 'else' proves nothing.

        @score: the score to label.
        @yields: the label, read as a plain string.
    ##
    string function LabelFor(integer score)
        string? label;

        if score >= 90
            label = "excellent";
        else if score >= 50
            label = "a pass";
        else
            label = "not yet";
        end if

        # Every arm stored one, so this is a string here and needs no unwrapping.
        yield label;
    end function

    # @summary: Shows a store in every arm proving a value after them.
    function EveryWayThrough()
        Console.WriteLine("== every way through ==");

        Console.WriteLine("  95 -> " + Program.LabelFor(95));
        Console.WriteLine("  61 -> " + Program.LabelFor(61));
        Console.WriteLine("  12 -> " + Program.LabelFor(12));
    end function

    # ---- Where it stops ----------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: The three places a proof runs out.

        @remarks: A branch that may not run, a loop, and a try. Each is a point control could
        have reached another way, so what held on one way is not what holds at the join.

        A value stored in any of them is still there — 'Or' and 'Value' reach it — and what
        stops is only the reading of it as a plain value. That is the trade: the compiler says
        no where it cannot be sure, and the two members are how a program says it is sure.
    ##
    function WhereItStops()
        Console.WriteLine("== where it stops ==");

        integer? fromABranch;

        if 1 == 2
            fromABranch = 5;
        end if

        # The store may not have happened, so this asks rather than assumes.
        Console.WriteLine("  from a branch: " + fromABranch.Or(0));

        integer? fromALoop;

        loop each value in {10, 20}
            fromALoop = value;
        end loop

        # A set may be empty, so a loop proves nothing either.
        Console.WriteLine("  from a loop:   " + fromALoop.Or(0));

        integer? fromATry;

        try
            fromATry = 30;
        catch Exception _
            Console.WriteLine("  unreachable");
        end try

        # An exception leaves the body from anywhere in it, this line included.
        Console.WriteLine("  from a try:    " + fromATry.Or(0));
    end function

    # ---- What a closure takes away -----------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: A name a kept function assigns is never narrowed at all.

        @remarks: Only a local or a parameter is ever narrowed, and only while nothing else
        can reach it. A field never qualifies, because any call in between could replace it.
        The moment a lambda assigns a local, the local is in the same position — the lambda
        holds the name and may be called at any point, so a proof made before a call says
        nothing about after it.

        Checking such a name does not help, and the compiler says so (PC0345) rather than
        sending its author to write a check that changes nothing. Copying it into a local
        does help: one nothing else holds sits still.
    ##
    function WhatAClosureTakesAway()
        Console.WriteLine("== what a closure takes away ==");

        integer? counted;
        counted = 7;

        delegate() forget = function()
            counted = Program.Nothing();
        end function;

        # Before the call and after it, the name is read the same way — because the compiler
        # cannot know which calls run, only that one of them could.
        Console.WriteLine("  before: " + counted.Or(0));
        forget();
        Console.WriteLine("  after:  " + counted.Or(0));

        # A copy nothing else holds narrows as any local does.
        integer? held = counted;

        if held.HasValue()
            Console.WriteLine("  held " + (held + 1));
        else
            Console.WriteLine("  the copy is empty too, and reads as a plain value inside a check");
        end if
    end function

    # @summary: An optional holding nothing, for the examples above.
    integer? function Nothing()
        integer? empty;
        yield empty;
    end function

end model
